# Proportion of junior professors in replicated studies in Psychology

**Population (primary).** File A `meta-regression_study_data_heterogeneity_in_replication_projects.csv`,
filter `project == "rpp"` = the Psychology replication project (RPP). One row = one original/replication (O/R)
pair. Study-level roll-up rows (`aggregated == True`, which exist only for ml1/ml3) are excluded before
filtering, so no study is double counted; rpp rows carry `aggregated = NaN`, so the exclusion is a no-op for rpp.
N = **97** unique `id`s; `seniority.r` and `seniority.o` have **0 missing**, so the denominator for the
"usable seniority" proportion is the full 97.

**Headline.** Replication line `seniority.r`, junior = Assistant Professor + Assistant (D2):
**38/97 = 39.2% (Wilson 95% CI 30.1%-49.1%)**.

**Alternative reading quoted in the hypothesis** (`replicated == 1`, n = 35):
**16/35 = 45.7% (Wilson 95% CI 30.5%-61.8%)**.

**Junior definition (a stated assumption, not a dataset field).** Tier order assumed
Professor > Associate Professor > Assistant Professor > Assistant > Researcher;
D1 = {Assistant Professor}, **D2 = {Assistant Professor, Assistant} (headline)**,
D3 = {Assistant Professor, Assistant, Researcher}; Researcher is excluded from the headline as non-professorial.

